A||||6||justme||headlights||||5||
Z||000000||justme||05-23-2001||01:36 PM||||I have read that the part in the 87-88 headlight motor that wears out is the bushing, and that you can get a replacement bushing from ecklers corvette for $5.  how hard is it to get into the motor and replace the bushing?  will the corvette part work?||||reg||5
Z||000001||thomas_l||05-23-2001||02:15 PM||||There's 3 bushings in there. I don't know if the Vette item is the same part, but I ordered a kit from Rodney Dickman to do both sides on mine for $8 + shipping. I really only need to do the pass side, but I figured I'd get the set for the spare. I haven't gotten it yet (too soon), but I'm sure to make a post about it after the job is done. I have already disassembled mine twice trying to clean/lubricate - the assembly isn't that bad. The only challenge is holding the brushes in when reassembling. Other than that you just need to remember what came apart to get it back together. Until my order comes in I get to watch the headlight dance it's way up and slam shut  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||05-23-2001||02:28 PM||||You want rodneys kit and get the one for $10 and just do both of them.<P>The drivers one goes a little faster than the passenger one. just follow the included directions. If you take your time you can usually save the cover gasket. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||05-23-2001||06:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by justme:<BR><B>the 87-88 headlight motor... how hard is it to get into the motor and replace the bushing?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>...about 10 times easier than working on the pre-1987 motors, where you must drill out the rivets that hold the motor housing together!<P>And I agree that rebuilding <B>both</B> motors is the way to do it.  Otherwise, if you're like me, you'll just lose the bushings somewhere, and/or forget how to do the job.  But do one motor at a time so that you can use the other as a reference, especially when it comes to reassembling the linkage.<P>FWIW, to hold the brushes while assembling the motor housing, I use a pair of paper clips, each bent into a right-angled "U" shape.  Simple to do - hard to describe, so I'll not try.<P>Rodney Dickman's kit probably has suggestions for retaining the brushes.  I've never used his kits - and I've also never read a discouraging word about him on this Forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <P>||||reg||12
Z||000004||Bob Englert||05-23-2001||08:36 PM||||If you are just changing the headlight bushings on the 87-88 headlight gears you don't need to get into the motors at all.  It is very easy to do, just take your time and follow the instructions in Rodney's kit.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ronnie||05-23-2001||09:13 PM||||I just repaired my passenger side. You do have to get into the motor on the passenger side because of the ridge on the gear. Just tie a sewing thread to the brush wire and tie it back so the armature can slip in. Then just cut the thread and pull it through thereby releasing the brushes inside on the armature. Simple job after doing it one time.||||reg||6
Z||000006||Bob Englert||05-23-2001||09:27 PM||||Ronnie:<P>You don't have to get into the motor on the passenger side.  Just cut the ridge off about 6 grooves of the gear and the worm gear will come out.  It is covered in Rodney's instructions.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
